Subject: Replace trailing-array struct hacks with checkable forms

Three libmdbx structures are allocated with room for more elements than their
trailing array declares, and then indexed past the declared bound:

    struct dpl       dp_t items[dpl_reserve_gap];   dirty-page list
    dml_t            da_t items[1];                 defrag map list
    ior_item_t       struct iovec sgv[1];           write-ring gather list

This is the pre-C99 "struct hack". clang treats a trailing array of any size as
flexible and says nothing, but gcc's -fsanitize=bounds-strict, which CRAN's
gcc-ASAN/gcc-UBSAN flavor uses, checks against the declared size. CRAN reported

    mdbx.c:30178:25: runtime error: index 1 out of bounds for type 'iovec [1]'

from osal_ioring_add() on every commit that gathered more than one segment
into a pwritev(). A local build with the same flags reproduced it, and a
harness that dirties more than 18 pages in one transaction also tripped
'dp_t [18]' at a dozen further sites that the package's tests do not reach.

`dpl` and `dml_t` become true flexible array members (C99 `[]`), which
bounds-strict leaves alone. Their size helpers used sizeof() to count the
header *including* the declared array, so each gains a *_header_size macro
that adds the old array back: the byte counts, and so every allocation, are
exactly what they were. Neither struct is ever embedded by value.

`sgv` cannot follow, because it shares an anonymous union with `single` and a
union cannot hold a flexible array member. It is indexed through an
`ior_sgv(item)` accessor instead, which casts through a pointer, so the access
is to heap memory the ring really owns rather than to a `[1]` array.

Semantically neutral. Not byte-identical: with __LINE__/__DATE__/__TIME__
pinned, gcc -O2 emits a different but equivalent sequence in one function
(osal_ioring_add loses a store-pair merge), and clang's diff is label and
outlined-function renumbering. Verified under clang
-fsanitize=undefined and gcc -fsanitize=address,undefined,bounds-strict with
MDBX_HAVE_PWRITEV=1: zero diagnostics before-and-after-reopen, where the
unpatched source gives the iovec and dp_t reports above.

Rejected alternative: -DMDBX_HAVE_PWRITEV=0 in Makevars. It removes the sgv
path by falling back to one pwrite() per contiguous run, which changes I/O
behaviour on every Linux and macOS install to silence a check, and does
nothing for `dpl`.

Rejected alternative: -fno-sanitize=bounds-strict. Not ours to set -- it is
CRAN's flavor -- and suppressing it would hide real out-of-bounds indexing.

